  'test-sharedbrain-route-guards.js', // v3.43.0: the five guards a LIVE two-instance run against a real private repo exposed, every one driven through the REAL express routers on a REAL server — no source-text assertions. F-01 CRITICAL: admin-token/rotate had NO proof of possession, so any connection on the machine could mint a token, and with it GET /:id/members hands out every fellow_id and POST /:id/revoke accepts it — a plain contributor could GDPR-erase the cohort admin, and the member card OFFERED the button. F-15: joining the same (repo, shared_domain) twice minted a second fellow_id for one person, so a revoke erased half their contributions while certifying a COMPLETE Article 17 erasure. Plus `shared-` finally reserved at domain CREATION (it was called reserved at three enforcement sites and was not), chat refusing to write a conversation into a read-only mirror (the live admin instance's mirror held one), and CURATOR_TEST_USER_DATA_DIR covering the LOG path — two throwaway instances had written their whole run into the maintainer's own ~/Library/Logs. Every refusal is paired with an ACCEPTANCE on the same route, and §1 asserts the STATE after a refusal, because a route that refuses in its response while still mutating is the shape that matters.
  'test-sharedbrain-queue-scope.js', // v3.43.0: the five DATA-LOSS defects from the same live run, driven through the real modules against real temp dirs. F-02: a multi-domain push loaded the connection ONCE and each pushDomain wrote pending_retry/permanent_skip as a REPLACEMENT, so domain B erased domain A's retry queue after last_push_at had advanced; those pages then fell out of both sets and the next push DIFFED them — their whole body arrived as prior version and routed to `stable_facts`, which nothing reads. Silent permanent loss under a green "Push complete", invisible because every existing fixture had a ONE-ELEMENT local_domains. F-08: the same queues were keyed on the page path with no domain qualifier, so two domains sharing a page name shared one strike counter. F-03: pullCollective ADOPTED an existing `shared-<slug>` domain and pruned it, and that slug comes from an UNSIGNED invite token. F-10: a fellow-supplied delta.path reached writePage unvalidated, and one unwritable path pins the watermark back — wedging synthesis for the whole cohort at the cost of one commit. F-11: appendAudit re-PUT a STALE string on a SHA conflict, silently dropping the other writer's erasure-log entry. F-12: the revocation audit stored an UNSALTED sha256 of the admin token in a file every contributor can read. §1/§2 assert the PAGES that survive, not only the bookkeeping — the bookkeeping is the mechanism, the pages are the harm.
