  'test-next-press-motion.js',    // The press vocabulary and its adoption. Before it the WHOLE /next tree carried two `:active` rules, both `transform: translateY(0.5px)` — measured live at 0.4716px, sub-pixel at 1x — while the rail, the list rows, the settings nav, the theme segments and the scope pills returned `transform: none` on a real mousedown. §2 is the AMPLITUDE RATCHET: every transform inside an `:active` rule must read a press token, so a return to a literal (or a THIRD amplitude beside the two that exist) fails rather than being plausible. §3 enumerates every button-role element FROM DISK and ratchets coverage; it also asserts it can still SEE an uncovered control, because total coverage would mean the resolver broke. §4 asserts the reduced-motion decision PER ELEMENT — presses disabled, the selection edges keeping their position (transform: none there would delete the accent bar, i.e. remove information rather than motion), and the ingest progress ring asserted NOT to be disabled, since it is the only liveness cue in a multi-minute paid write. §5 forbids two keyframes sharing a body — chat-spin/mcpwFadeIn/sbwFadeIn were byte-equivalent copies and are retired. §6 catches the bug no other check here can: a press background that TIES with its own `:hover` on specificity and is declared before it never paints, which happened twice while writing the CSS. NOT ENFORCED and said so: cascade is resolved only for same-file ties, specificity is counted not computed, perceptibility needs a browser, and a press inherited from an ancestor is counted as uncovered rather than exempted.
