  'test-next-header-adoption.js', // THE HOLE THE VIEW-HEADER GUARD LEFT OPEN, and the instance that proves it was worth closing. test-next-view-header.js guards adoption in two layers, and both are blind the same way: §8 names call sites in a HARDCODED list of four views, and §9b — the class guard that walks every view on disk — opens `if (!src.includes('renderViewHeader(')) continue;`. Together they prove "every ADOPTER behaves" and "these four are ADOPTERS", and say nothing about a fifth view painting `<h1 class="view-title">` plus a paragraph by hand. So this suite asks the question from the other side: enumerate every view FROM DISK and forbid the component's OWN output classes (`view-title`, `sidebar-title`) as raw string literals, because a hand-rolled header is one that will accept a paragraph the moment someone adds one. Its first run found four more views and a LIVE defect: chat.js, the app's default view, still floats "Chat needs at least one domain to talk to" directly under its <h1>. Those are carried in a PENDING ratchet whose every entry must MATCH SOMETHING (test-next-loading-gate.js's discipline) — the budget may shrink and never grow, so fixing one turns its entry stale and goes red rather than passing quietly. §3 exempts the three DIALOG files on EVIDENCE rather than opinion: each takes its accessible name from `aria-labelledby` pointing at an id on its own heading, and the component is measured to emit no id and no `titleId`, so adopting would leave the dialog unnamed — an exemption that re-opens by itself if that ever changes. §6 is the `title=` ratchet: the two strings carrying a fact available nowhere else were PROMOTED to visible notes (both qualify whether the reader should trust the document), and the three survivors are ISO stamps behind humanised ages, which hide no fact. §0 proves the comment-stripper strips before anything trusts it, in both directions — a `//`-commented call site satisfies a positive scan, and a guard in this same push